{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.088cdb9f98","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-393","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Redfern is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1912.</li><li>Its surface area is 422 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Northam is in eastern Australia. It was first surveyed in 2003.</li><li>Its surface area is 363 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Redfern and Lake Northam.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Lake Redfern and Lake Northam are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Redfern is in eastern Poland, and Lake Northam is in eastern Australia.","The surface area of Lake Redfern (422 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Northam (363 square kilometers).","Lake Redfern was first surveyed in 1912, while Lake Northam was first surveyed in 2003."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.64f6175cb5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-393","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Redfern is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1912.</li><li>Its surface area is 422 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Northam is in eastern Australia. It was first surveyed in 2003.</li><li>Its surface area is 363 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two lakes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Redfern (422 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Northam (363 square kilometers).","Lake Redfern, which is in eastern Poland, is one of several reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Redfern was first surveyed in 1912, while Lake Northam was first surveyed in 2003.","Lake Redfern is in eastern Poland, and Lake Northam is in eastern Australia."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4f8d742554","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-394","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Halden is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1911.</li><li>Its surface area is 854 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Dunmore is in central Spain. It was first surveyed in 1978.</li><li>Its surface area is 658 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two lakes were first surveyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Lake Halden and Lake Dunmore are reservoirs created by dams.","The surface area of Lake Halden (854 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Dunmore (658 square kilometers).","Lake Halden is in eastern Poland, and Lake Dunmore is in central Spain.","Lake Halden was first surveyed in 1911, while Lake Dunmore was first surveyed in 1978."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7f2c7d2fcb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-9","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Rahman is an ecologist.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Rahman compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Rahman found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Rahman concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ecologist Sofia Rahman set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.","To investigate the question, Rahman compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Rahman concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","Rahman found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f69f9e2f94","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-14","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Novak is a psychologist.</li><li>Novak wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","Because students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly, Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","To investigate the question, Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7fac35924b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-18","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Haddad is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Haddad grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Haddad found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Haddad concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.","Haddad concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","Because fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer, Haddad concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","To investigate the question, Haddad grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c2b9a31d0b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-29","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Varga is a materials scientist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Varga coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Varga found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Varga concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Varga found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","To investigate the question, Varga coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.","Materials scientist Tomás Varga set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8ab1fae15c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-50","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Delacroix is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Delacroix asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Delacroix found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days, Delacroix concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","To investigate the question, Delacroix asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Delacroix concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","Delacroix found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.72e7bdf897","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-52","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Duarte is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Duarte asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Duarte found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Duarte concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Duarte concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","Duarte found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","To investigate the question, Duarte asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Nutrition researcher Diego Duarte set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3f8c1d5f20","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-56","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Haddad is a zoologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Zoologist Beatriz Haddad set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.","Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","To investigate the question, Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1a64d6d167","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-62","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Halvorsen is an astronomer.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Halvorsen measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Halvorsen found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Halvorsen found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","Astronomer Beatriz Halvorsen set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Halvorsen concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.17a182837d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-65","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Castellano is an astronomer.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Castellano measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Castellano found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Castellano concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","Astronomer Leila Castellano set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","Castellano found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","To investigate the question, Castellano measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ee8920ac3b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-70","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Takeda is a linguist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Takeda gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Takeda found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Takeda concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Takeda concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Linguist Chen Takeda set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.","Takeda found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.210860a77b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-83","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Nakamura is a botanist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.</li><li>Nakamura grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.</li><li>Nakamura found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Nakamura grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.","Botanist Zara Nakamura set out to learn whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.","Nakamura found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.","Nakamura concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4dc2466140","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-89","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Moreau is an urban planner.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Moreau measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Moreau found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Moreau concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Moreau measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.","Moreau found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.","Because streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler, Moreau concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","Moreau concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8ea8f1b3f4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-92","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Castellano is a hydrologist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Castellano compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Castellano found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Castellano concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.","To investigate the question, Castellano compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.","Hydrologist Ingrid Castellano set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","Castellano found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.079a8d8597","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-100","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Sato is an education researcher.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.</li><li>Sato had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.</li><li>Sato found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.</li><li>Sato concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Sato concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.","To investigate the question, Sato had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.","Sato found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.","Because children in the read-aloud group learned more new words, Sato concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.06d3039901","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-105","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Kowalski is a chemist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Kowalski placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Kowalski found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Chemist Amara Kowalski set out to learn whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.","Kowalski concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.","To investigate the question, Kowalski placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Kowalski found that most samples broke apart within four months."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.88e29337ba","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-111","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Takeda is a primatologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Takeda observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Takeda found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Takeda concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier, Takeda concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.","Takeda concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.","Takeda found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","To investigate the question, Takeda observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.01a2278a6a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-113","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Delacroix is a primatologist.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Delacroix observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Delacroix found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Delacroix found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","To investigate the question, Delacroix observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Delacroix concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.","Because young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier, Delacroix concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b2d55409fc","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-115","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Novak is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Novak wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Novak asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Novak found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Novak concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Novak concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","Sleep researcher Ravi Novak set out to learn whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.","Novak found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.","To investigate the question, Novak asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.074a5e3325","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-115","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Novak is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Novak wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Novak asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Novak found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Novak concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep, Novak concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","Novak concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","To investigate the question, Novak asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Novak found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ec74969920","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-123","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Ferreira is an ornithologist.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.</li><li>Ferreira measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.</li><li>Ferreira found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings, Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.","Ferreira found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.","Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.","To investigate the question, Ferreira measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1fc99183eb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-128","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Moreau is a public health researcher.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Moreau counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Moreau found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Moreau concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.","Moreau found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","Public health researcher Zara Moreau set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.","To investigate the question, Moreau counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6bc9242785","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-132","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Okafor is an archaeologist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Okafor analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Okafor found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Okafor concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Archaeologist Amara Okafor set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.","Okafor found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","To investigate the question, Okafor analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Okafor concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7b1142fd49","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-133","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Viktor Takeda is an archaeologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Takeda analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Takeda found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Takeda concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Takeda found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","Because many pots were made from clay found only far inland, Takeda concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","Takeda concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.edf6da2805","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-144","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Castellano is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Castellano fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Castellano found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Castellano concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Marine ecologist Ravi Castellano set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","To investigate the question, Castellano fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Castellano concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","Castellano found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3c4e197d20","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-168","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Viktor Kowalski is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Kowalski added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Kowalski found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Kowalski added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.","Kowalski concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","Kowalski found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.","Forest ecologist Viktor Kowalski set out to learn whether fungi help trees share nutrients."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1265f37a60","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-172","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Oyelaran is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Oyelaran wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Oyelaran added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Oyelaran found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Oyelaran concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Oyelaran added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.","Oyelaran concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","Because the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks, Oyelaran concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","Oyelaran found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.365c596b9d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-174","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Okafor is a music researcher.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Okafor tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Okafor found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Okafor concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Okafor tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Music researcher Sofia Okafor set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","Okafor concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","Okafor found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."}
